a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/899391"} "Clarence, Greve von Rosen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5005199"} "Cornelis Baard"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The " [:strong {} "Official Report"] " lists 450 paintings, drawings, and graphic works, but those details do not match with the Art Exhibition catalog. There were 49 artists from 12 countries in the paintings category. Among the entrants was " [:a {:href "/athletes/22190"} "Edgar Seligman"] ", a five-time Olympian in fencing (1906-1924), who won three silver medals. A number of very prominent painters took part, including " [:a {:href "/athletes/920724"} "Max Slevogt"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/920604"} "Otto Dill"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/920627"} "George Grosz"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/921760"} "Max Liebermann"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/920582"} "Willi Baumeister"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/921534"} "Hunt Diederich"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/920525"} "Gerald Spencer Pryse"] ", among others."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold medal was presented to Dutch painter " [:a {:href "/athletes/921076"} "Isaac Israëls"] " for his painting " [:em {} "De rode Ruiter"] " (The Red Rider), in which the rider, in his red skirt, has stopped his horse. Art historian Richard Stanton noted that the painting was produced on behalf of J. J. Leeuwenburgh, the Lord sitting on his horse “Tristan.” The client did not like the painting, so Israëls sent it to the art competition. This is doubtful because, according to the Dutch catalogue, two versions of the “Cavalier Rouge” existed before. One belonged to a Mr. Glerum from Amsterdam, the other to Lord Scherjon from Utrecht."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Another famous painter entered in 1928 was " [:a {:href "/athletes/920500"} "Laura Knight"] ", who won the silver medal with her painting " [:em {} "Boxers"] ", although art historian Bernard Kramer stated the boxer was not the best painting by Ms. Knight, noting, “Despite successful presentation of contrasting foreground and background with the dark auditorium and the boxers fighting in the full glare, the seething atmosphere of the boxing ring does not seem to be captured.”"] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medal was awarded to German painter " [:a {:href "/athletes/920656"} "Walther Klemm"] " for " [:em {} "Eislauf auf dem Tennisplatz"] " (Skaters on the Tennis Court). The painting was acquired by the German Reich Ministry of the Interior after the exhibition."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Translated Title"] [:th {} "Original Title"] [:th {} "Title in Olympic Report or Catalogue"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "472"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/921076"} "Isaac Israëls"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "The Red Rider I"] [:td {} "De rode rijder / De roode ruiter / Ruiter in roode rok / Ruiter met de rode jas"] [:td {} "Cavalier Rouge, (p.) (Appartient à M. Scherjon, Utrecht)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "321"]